<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-therapy/foundational/what-art-therapy-is">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Art therapy is mental-health practice through image-making. Its central claim: the creative
    process — choosing, shaping, risking, revising — engages and expresses material that talk
    alone often cannot reach, and the trained therapist works with both the process and the
    product, clinically.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="process, media, images, methods, ethics" widget="equationStep" config={processCfg} />

  <h2>Media choice is clinical choice</h2>
  <p>
    Hand a client wet watercolour and control dissolves; hand them a fine pen and control returns.
    Therapists use this deliberately: fluid, resistive media for clients who need to practise
    letting go; structured media for those who need containment and mastery. In trauma work the
    sequencing matters — starting with controllable media before approaching the uncontainable.
  </p>

  <h2>Assessment: reading process carefully</h2>
  <p>
    The field's history includes over-reading images ("draw-a-person" formulas now largely
    retired). Modern practice assesses the <em>process</em> — hesitation, pressure, erasing,
    spacing, narrative while working — with cultural humility, because symbols are not universal.
    The image is data; it is never a diagnosis by itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it practises</h2>
  <p>
    Children (who often cannot verbalise trauma), medical patients (pain, grief, identity after
    diagnosis), older adults (dementia care, where image-making reaches preserved abilities),
    veterans, and group settings in schools and clinics. The ATCB credential exists to bind all
    of it to standards: supervision, ethics of image confidentiality, and honest evidence claims.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ATCB content outline; Malchiodi, <em>Handbook of Art Therapy</em>; American Art Therapy Association ethical principles.</p>
</TopicPage>
